Blockers from round 1, verified closed:
1. HIGH false stamp-less claim: closed. Brief 150-159 now states P-014/P-015 (qa-lead) appear until 2026-10-03T11:03Z, other eleven out of window; acceptance 323-327 confines byte-identity to the fixture. Matches my grep.
2. MED stamp parse: closed. 129-134: last token must match the anchored ISO-Z regex, stamp <= now tested separately from daysBetween (whose clamp I confirmed, context.ts:36). One parse route only (the same "last token" feeds the window test and the cap sort). Row 5(b) injects 2099 through the real verb (newline reply, as I probed), 5(c) covers "1" and "2099"; residual 7 records the under-delivery. Tokens "1"/"2099" cannot pass the regex, so the lenient-Date/TZ route is gone.
3. MED party rule: closed. 125-128 is (from===sella && to===patron) || (to===sella && from===patron) as one conjunct with test 2 (sella !== patron), so a patron-to-patron or sella-to-sella file cannot match either arm; row 5(d) pins both directions. Remaining route: a file-writing sella can still forge "from: <victim> to: patron" (see A1), which no rule on resolved petitiones can prevent; residual 7 states the carry is not an authenticity control.
4. MED growth: closed for the real officina and the fixture. Cap 136-142: newest 3, 1000-char tail, pending asks first. Measured: eng-lead studio context is 848 tokens after truncation, qa-lead 2357, producer 2089, architect 2137, sample eng-lead 624; worst-case carry is ~850 tokens, far under the 4000 budget. Row 5(e): without the cap the six 3000-char bodies (~4500 tokens) plus base (~600) exceed 4000, truncation drops 8,7,6 then 5, so the A-20 header and three-header assertions fail; the cap makes them pass. The row bites. New holes: none blocking.

Advisories (not blocking, no fix required before sign-off):
A1. Cap displacement (LOW): a file-writing sella can forge up to three newer stamped "from: <victim> to: patron" petitiones, pushing the genuine reply out of the newest 3 and showing ~750 tokens of its text, data-wrapped, in the victim's boot. Same trust class as residual 7 and as acta decisions reaching every sella (context.ts:192-199); consider naming it in residual 8 ("cap is silent" also means a forged entry can displace a real one).
A2. Wording (LOW), brief 141-142: "cannot ... cause truncation to drop the pending ask" is stronger than the code. The loop (context.ts:220-226) drops priorities 8, 7, 6 before 5, and pending asks are unbounded and share the section, so the guarantee holds only while the base context minus 6-8 stays under ~3250 tokens (true today by the measurements above). Suggest "in practice" or the threshold.
A3. Calendar overflow (LOW): the regex admits "2026-02-31T00:00:00Z", which V8 parses as Mar 3 (probed), shifting a forged stamp by days; bounded by stamp <= now and the window. A toISOString round-trip equality check would close it; optional.
A4. Tail trim (LOW): a reply longer than 1000 chars cuts the head of its own stamp line, so the timestamp is lost from the view while the text survives. Acceptable.
A5. Row 5(e)'s "tokens <= 4000" is weak alone (a truncated output also satisfies it); the A-20 header and exact three-header assertions carry the row. Fine as written.
Harness (env pin, timeout, dereference, UTC glob) matches precedent. No new findings in rows 1-4, 6.
